# fields: date, title, genre, year, story, facts[], art, kind, extra
E = [
("04-01","The 'Funky Drummer' break that built a thousand hits","Funk","1970","Clyde Stubblefield's drum break on James Brown's 'Funky Drummer' was improvised filler. It became one of the most sampled recordings in history.\n\nThe break arrives about five and a half minutes in, after Brown tells the band to give the drummer some. What follows is roughly eight bars of Stubblefield alone, playing a pattern built on ghost notes — the quiet, almost-inaudible strokes between the main hits that give the groove its shuffle. He has said he was not doing anything special and did not think about it again.\n\nWhat made it so useful to producers is the ghost notes. A pattern with that much internal detail can be chopped, slowed, sped up, or rearranged and still sound like a human being playing, which is exactly what a drum machine could not do in 1986.\n\nPublic Enemy, Run-DMC, LL Cool J, Sinéad O'Connor, George Michael, and hundreds of others built records on it. It is among the two or three most sampled recordings ever made.\n\nStubblefield received no sampling royalties. He was a session player on a work-for-hire basis and had no claim under the law as it stood. He said repeatedly that he did not object to people using the music — what bothered him was not being named.\n\nHe died in 2017.",["Public Enemy, Run-DMC, LL Cool J and George Michael all used it","Stubblefield was never paid sampling royalties and said he didn't mind the music, only the credit"],"drums","sample",{"src":"James Brown - Funky Drummer (1970)","hits":["Public Enemy - Fight the Power","Run-DMC - Run's House","LL Cool J - Mama Said Knock You Out"]}),
("04-02","Which 'Respect' wins: Otis or Aretha?","Soul","1967","Otis Redding wrote it in 1965 as a working man's plea. Aretha Franklin rebuilt it in 1967 as a demand, adding the spelling hook and her sisters' backing vocals.\n\nThe two versions are worth hearing back to back because almost nothing changes on paper. Same title, largely the same words, similar tempo. Redding's is a man coming home from work asking to be treated properly in his own house — the tone is tired and slightly pleading, and the arrangement pushes forward like someone making a case.\n\nFranklin's inverts the power relationship completely. The narrator is not asking. She is stating terms, and the spelled-out hook — invented in the studio with her sisters Carolyn and Erma — functions as an instruction rather than a chorus.\n\nThe arrangement does its share. King Curtis's saxophone solo quotes 'When Something Is Wrong with My Baby', the rhythm section drives rather than swings, and the whole thing was built quickly at Atlantic with Jerry Wexler and the Muscle Shoals players.\n\nRedding took it well and appears to have found it funny, joking at Monterey that the girl had taken his song away from him.\n\nMost covers disappear behind the original. This is one of the few cases where the second version became the definitive one, and the first became the interesting footnote.",["Redding joked she had stolen the song for good","Aretha's version was cut in a single afternoon session"],"mic","versus",{"a":"Otis Redding (1965)","b":"Aretha Franklin (1967)"}),
("04-03","Nile Rodgers' guitar is on more hits than you think","Disco","1979","The Chic guitarist's clipped 'chucking' style powered his own band, then Bowie's 'Let's Dance', Madonna's 'Like a Virgin' and Daft Punk's 'Get Lucky'.\n\nThe technique is deceptively simple to describe and hard to do. He plays tightly muted sixteenth-note chords with almost no sustain, so the guitar functions as a percussion instrument that also supplies harmony. The notes are short enough that they occupy almost no space in a mix, which is why a Rodgers part can sit in a dense arrangement without fighting anything.\n\nHe calls the approach chucking, and he plays almost all of it on the same 1960 Stratocaster he refers to as the Hitmaker.\n\nWith Bernard Edwards he wrote and produced not just Chic but Sister Sledge's catalogue and Diana Ross's 'Upside Down', and the Chic rhythm section is one of the most sampled and interpolated in existence — 'Good Times' alone underpins 'Rapper's Delight' and a long list of records after it.\n\nHis second career as a producer for hire is arguably larger than his first. Bowie's Let's Dance, Madonna's Like a Virgin, Duran Duran, INXS, and much later Daft Punk's 'Get Lucky' all carry the same guitar.\n\nHe has survived cancer twice and has been unusually candid in public about both his health and his sobriety.",["He uses a 1960 Stratocaster he calls 'The Hitmaker'","He and Bernard Edwards wrote Sister Sledge's catalogue too"],"guitar","producer",{}),
("04-04","Marvin Gaye's 'Inner City Blues' hid a second bassline","Soul","1971","Bassist Bob Babbitt and James Jamerson both appear across the album, and Gaye layered a second bass an octave apart to thicken the low end.\n\nDoubling a bass part an octave up is a trick that solves a specific problem: sub-bass frequencies vanish on small speakers, so a second bass carrying the same line higher keeps the melodic movement audible on a car radio while the low octave supplies the weight. It is why the What's Going On low end feels enormous and still reads clearly.\n\nThe Funk Brothers were Motown's uncredited house band, and Jamerson was their most consequential member. He played most of his parts with a single index finger — nicknamed the Hook — which forced a particular kind of economy and produced lines that are melodically independent rather than simply following the root.\n\nThe story about him recording lying flat on his back is one of the better-known Motown anecdotes and comes from a session where he had been drinking; whether it happened once or often depends on who is telling it.\n\nThe Funk Brothers went uncredited on Motown sleeves for years despite playing on more number ones than the Beatles, the Beach Boys, and Elvis combined. Recognition arrived largely after most of them had died.",["Jamerson famously recorded lying flat on his back","He played most parts with a single index finger, nicknamed 'The Hook'"],"guitar","story",{}),
("04-05","The Amen break is six seconds long and built three genres","Funk","1969","Chopped and sped up, the Winstons' break became the engine of jungle, drum and bass and UK hardcore, often at double its original tempo.\n\nWhat British producers did with it in the early nineties went well beyond looping. The break was sliced into its individual drum hits and reassembled into entirely new patterns, then time-stretched to tempos around 160 to 180 beats per minute — roughly double the original — while keeping the pitch usable. That editing process is the actual compositional technique of jungle, and it required the sampler to be treated as an instrument rather than a playback device.\n\nThe result is a genre in which almost every record is built from the same six seconds and almost none of them sound alike.\n\nIts reach extends far past dance music. It appears on N.W.A's Straight Outta Compton, the Prodigy's 'Firestarter', an Oasis single, television idents, and an enormous volume of advertising.\n\nNobody in the Winstons was ever paid for any of it. Richard Spencer, the band's leader and the copyright holder, was aware and had no practical route to enforcement.\n\nA crowdfunding campaign in the UK raised a modest sum for him in 2015 — a voluntary gesture from producers rather than anything the industry required.",["Producers slice it into individual hits and rebuild new patterns","A crowdfunding campaign later raised money for the surviving band member"],"drums","sample",{"src":"The Winstons - Amen, Brother (1969)","hits":["N.W.A - Straight Outta Compton","The Prodigy - Firestarter","Oasis - D'You Know What I Mean?"]}),
("04-06","Which 'Hallelujah' wins: Cohen or Buckley?","Folk","1984","Leonard Cohen wrote around 80 verses and released a restrained gospel-tinged version. Jeff Buckley's 1994 reading, learned from John Cale's cover, made it a standard.\n\nCohen's writing process was famously agonising. He worked on the song for years and produced something in the region of eighty verses, and he described a low point spent on a hotel room floor in his underwear, banging his head, unable to finish it. The version he released in 1984 is restrained, organ-backed, and closer to a hymn than a ballad. His label initially refused to release the album it came from in the United States.\n\nThe chain of transmission matters. John Cale recorded a stripped-down piano version for a Cohen tribute album, selecting a different set of verses from the many Cohen sent him. Jeff Buckley learned the song from Cale's version rather than Cohen's, which is why the words most people know are Cale's edit.\n\nBuckley's recording is essentially one electric guitar and a voice, largely captured live, and it treats the song as intimate and sexual rather than liturgical.\n\nThe song's subsequent ubiquity — talent shows, funerals, films — has worn it down considerably, which is a strange fate for something that took a decade to write and sat ignored for another.",["Cohen spent years writing it, reportedly banging his head on a hotel floor","Buckley's take is a single guitar and voice, recorded largely live"],"guitar","versus",{"a":"Leonard Cohen (1984)","b":"Jeff Buckley (1994)"}),
("04-07","Billie Holiday recorded a protest song no label would touch","Jazz","1939","Columbia refused 'Strange Fruit', so Holiday cut it for tiny Commodore Records. Clubs made her close with it, lights out, no encore.\n\nThe lyric began as a poem by Abel Meeropol, a Jewish schoolteacher from the Bronx writing under the name Lewis Allan, after he saw a photograph of a lynching. It is an unflinching piece of writing that describes what it describes without metaphor beyond the title itself.\n\nColumbia would not touch it, fearing the reaction in the South and from radio. The label granted Holiday a one-off release to record it for Commodore, a small jazz label run out of a record shop.\n\nThe performance protocol at Café Society, the integrated Greenwich Village club where she introduced it, was strict and deliberate: it closed the set, service stopped, the room went dark except for a single spotlight, and there was no encore. The audience was given nothing to follow it with.\n\nHolliday said singing it made her physically ill, and she performed it for the rest of her life anyway.\n\nIt sold well and was widely censored. It is frequently cited as the first significant protest song of the era, and it made her a target of federal harassment that continued until her death in 1959, at forty-four.",["The lyric began as a poem by a Bronx schoolteacher","She said singing it made her physically ill every time"],"mic","story",{}),
("04-08","Brooklyn's Bed-Stuy gave hip-hop its densest block","Hip-Hop","1988","Within a few square blocks, Big Daddy Kane, Jay-Z, Biggie, Lil' Kim and Masta Ace all emerged, a concentration unmatched anywhere.\n\nThe concentration is genuinely unusual. Scenes normally cluster around an institution — a club, a label, a studio — and this one clustered around a neighbourhood, with park jams and street corners functioning as the proving ground long before any label was paying attention.\n\nBig Daddy Kane set the technical standard. His delivery in the late eighties was faster and more intricate than almost anything around it, with multisyllabic rhyme and breath control that other rappers openly studied, and he was a direct influence on Jay-Z, who has said as much repeatedly.\n\nWhat that produced was a local competitive environment where the bar was set by people you could actually run into. Biggie, Jay-Z, Lil' Kim, and Masta Ace all came up inside a few square blocks of it.\n\nThe economics underneath were not benign. Bed-Stuy in the late eighties was dealing with disinvestment, the crack epidemic, and aggressive policing, and the same conditions that produced the music produced a great deal of harm.\n\nThe neighbourhood is now substantially gentrified, and the blocks in question are unaffordable to the families that produced all of this.",["Kane's rapid-fire delivery set the technical bar for the era","Local park jams were the proving ground before any label interest"],"turntable","story",{}),
("04-09","Rick Rubin produced by taking things away","Hip-Hop","1986","Rubin's method was subtraction: strip the arrangement until only the essential remains. He applied it to Run-DMC, Slayer, Johnny Cash and Adele.\n\nThe early hip-hop work is the clearest demonstration. Where contemporaries were layering samples and synths, Rubin stripped tracks down to a hard drum machine pattern, a guitar, and a voice — Run-DMC's records sound enormous because there is almost nothing in them, so each element occupies the full width of the mix.\n\nHe cannot play an instrument to any useful standard and does not engineer. What he does is closer to editing: identifying which parts of a record are load-bearing and removing everything else, and getting a performance rather than a construction.\n\nThe American Recordings sessions with Johnny Cash are the most extreme version. A man in his sixties whose career had stalled, recorded with an acoustic guitar and essentially nothing else, singing covers of songs from outside his tradition. The results reset his reputation entirely in the last decade of his life.\n\nThe range is the striking part: Def Jam, founded in an NYU dorm room with Russell Simmons, then Slayer, the Beastie Boys, Red Hot Chili Peppers, System of a Down, Dixie Chicks, and Adele.\n\nThe common thread is not a sound. It is the willingness to say that most of what has been recorded should be deleted.",["He often cannot play any instrument on the records he makes","His Def Jam label started in an NYU dorm room"],"mixer","producer",{}),
("04-10","'Under Pressure' was a jam nobody planned","Rock","1981","Bowie and Queen were in the same Swiss studio and started improvising. The famous bassline came from John Deacon noodling during a break.\n\nThe two acts were working at Mountain Studios in Montreux at the same time, and what began as Bowie dropping in to add backing vocals to something else turned into an all-day improvisation. Neither party arrived with a song.\n\nThe origin of the bassline is genuinely disputed. Most accounts credit John Deacon playing it during a break; Brian May has suggested Bowie changed it; Deacon has given differing versions over the years. The likeliest explanation is that nobody was paying attention at the moment it happened because nobody knew it mattered yet.\n\nThe vocal was built by a method Bowie proposed: each singer went into the booth without hearing what the other had done, sang over the track, and the results were combined. That is why the two voices overlap and interrupt rather than trading verses politely.\n\nBoth parties later expressed reservations about the mix, having run out of patience by the end of the session.\n\nVanilla Ice used the bassline on 'Ice Ice Baby' in 1990, initially claimed it was different because of an added note, and eventually settled with credit and payment.",["Accounts differ on who actually invented the riff","Vanilla Ice later used it and initially denied the resemblance"],"guitar","story",{}),
("04-11","Which 'I Will Always Love You' wins: Dolly or Whitney?","Country","1974","Dolly Parton wrote it as a quiet goodbye to her mentor. Whitney Houston's 1992 version added the a cappella opening that producer David Foster fought for.\n\nParton's original is a country ballad in the truest sense — restrained, conversational, and addressed to a specific person, Porter Wagoner, whose show had made her and whose partnership she was leaving. It works because it does not escalate. The emotional force comes from what is being held back.\n\nHouston's version does the opposite and is equally correct on its own terms. The a cappella opening was David Foster's idea and was contested internally as too risky for radio; it survived, and it is the reason the record is instantly identifiable from its first two seconds. Kevin Costner has said he pushed for it.\n\nThe key change before the final chorus is one of the most conspicuous in pop and works because Houston had the instrument to justify it.\n\nParton had refused to hand publishing to Elvis Presley's manager twenty years earlier, which meant the enormous royalties from The Bodyguard came to her. She has described hearing the Houston version on the car radio for the first time and having to pull over.\n\nTwo readings of the same words, both definitive, which almost never happens.",["Parton has said she cried hearing Whitney's version on the radio","The a cappella intro was nearly cut for being too risky"],"mic","versus",{"a":"Dolly Parton (1974)","b":"Whitney Houston (1992)"}),
("04-12","The 'Think' break gave hip-hop its whistle","Funk","1972","Lyn Collins' 'Think (About It)', produced by James Brown, supplied the 'woo! yeah!' stabs heard on hundreds of records.\n\nThe useful material is two vocal exclamations and a drum break, and their value is entirely rhythmic. The stabs are short, percussive, and pitched high enough to cut through a dense mix, which makes them ideal punctuation — a producer can drop one at the end of a bar and it functions like a cymbal that happens to be a human voice.\n\nRob Base and DJ E-Z Rock's 'It Takes Two' is built almost entirely on it, and once that record hit in 1988 the sample became shorthand for a party record. It has been used so widely since that most listeners recognise it without ever having heard the original.\n\nLyn Collins sang for the James Brown revue and was billed as the Female Preacher. Her own career never reached the scale her voice warranted, which was a common outcome for singers inside Brown's organisation — he controlled the productions, the arrangements, and the release schedules.\n\nBrown's catalogue as a whole is the single largest source of sampled material in hip-hop, and the payment situation for the musicians who played on it was, throughout, poor.\n\nCollins died in 2005.",["Rob Base and DJ E-Z Rock built an entire hit on it","The vocal stabs are now shorthand for party records"],"drums","sample",{"src":"Lyn Collins - Think (About It) (1972)","hits":["Rob Base - It Takes Two","Janet Jackson - Alright","Kanye West - Hey Mama"]}),
("04-13","Carole King wrote hits in Brooklyn before singing them","Pop","1942","Born in Manhattan and raised in Brooklyn, King wrote dozens of standards with Gerry Goffin before 'Tapestry' made her a performer.\n\nShe attended James Madison High School in Brooklyn and was writing professionally as a teenager. 'Will You Love Me Tomorrow', written with Gerry Goffin when she was around seventeen, became the first number one by a Black girl group when the Shirelles recorded it — and it is a remarkably adult piece of writing about the morning after, which is not what the market expected from a teenager in 1960.\n\nThe Goffin–King partnership produced an extraordinary run: 'The Loco-Motion', 'Up on the Roof', '(You Make Me Feel Like) A Natural Woman', 'Pleasant Valley Sunday'. She wrote the music, he wrote the lyrics, and they worked inside the Brill Building system alongside a floor of direct competitors.\n\nThe move to performing came after the marriage ended and after the industry had shifted toward artists writing their own material. Tapestry, in 1971, was one of the best-selling albums of the decade and reframed her entirely — the same songs, sung by the person who wrote them, at a piano.\n\nHer version of 'A Natural Woman' arrived four years after Aretha Franklin's, which is an unusual direction of travel.",["She attended James Madison High School in Brooklyn","'Will You Love Me Tomorrow' was written when she was 17"],"piano","writer",{}),
("04-14","Bernie Worrell made the synthesizer funky","Funk","1975","Parliament-Funkadelic's keyboardist treated the Moog as a bass instrument, inventing the squelching low end that shaped G-funk two decades later.\n\nThe innovation was conceptual before it was technical. Synthesizers in the mid-seventies were treated as lead or texture instruments; Worrell used the Minimoog as a bass, playing lines with portamento and filter sweeps that a bass guitar physically cannot produce. The result is the sliding, rubbery low end on records like 'Flash Light', which is one of the most influential sounds in American popular music.\n\nHe was classically trained. He began piano at three, wrote a concerto as a child, and attended the New England Conservatory, which is not the background one assumes for the P-Funk keyboard chair — and it is precisely why the harmony on those records is more sophisticated than the party framing suggests.\n\nDr. Dre's nineties productions lean directly on this vocabulary. G-funk is essentially the P-Funk synth bass and lead-line language slowed down and given more space, and Worrell's playing is sampled and interpolated throughout it.\n\nHe later worked with Talking Heads, appearing prominently in Stop Making Sense, which put him in front of an entirely different audience.\n\nHe died in 2016. The financial arrangements around the P-Funk catalogue were contested for decades and he saw comparatively little from it.",["He was classically trained and wrote a concerto as a child","Dr. Dre's nineties productions lean directly on his vocabulary"],"synth","producer",{}),
("04-15","Which 'Tainted Love' wins: Gloria Jones or Soft Cell?","Pop","1964","Gloria Jones cut a fast Northern Soul stomper that flopped. Soft Cell slowed it to a synth crawl in 1981 and had a worldwide hit.\n\nJones's original is fast, brass-driven, and relentlessly forward-moving — a soul record built for dancing that failed commercially on release and then found a second life in the UK. Northern Soul clubs in the north of England were built on exactly this kind of obscure American single, and the record became a floor favourite there for over a decade before anyone thought to cover it.\n\nSoft Cell's version keeps the melody almost intact and inverts everything else. The tempo drops, the arrangement becomes a sparse synthesizer pattern with an enormous amount of space, and the vocal shifts from defiant to exhausted. The same words describing a relationship gone toxic read completely differently at half the energy.\n\nIt became one of the biggest-selling singles of 1981 and held a long run on the American chart.\n\nJones later played keyboards in Marc Bolan's band and was his partner; she was driving the car in the 1977 crash that killed him.\n\nThe pair is a useful demonstration that arrangement is not decoration. Nothing meaningful changed in the writing and the song became a different song.",["The song was a UK club favourite for years before the cover","Soft Cell's version keeps the melody but inverts the energy entirely"],"synth","versus",{"a":"Gloria Jones (1964)","b":"Soft Cell (1981)"}),
("04-16","Dub plates were one-off records made for a single night","Reggae","1975","Sound systems cut exclusive acetates so rivals could not play their tunes. The discs wore out after a few dozen plays.\n\nAn acetate is an aluminium disc coated in soft lacquer, cut directly by a lathe rather than pressed from a stamper. It can be produced as a single copy within hours, and it degrades audibly with every play — a few dozen passes and the high end is gone.\n\nFor a sound system operator that was the entire appeal. Exclusivity, not fidelity, was the point. Having a tune nobody else could play was a competitive advantage in a culture where two systems set up on the same street and the crowd decided who won, and it was worth paying a studio to cut a version with a vocalist naming your system directly.\n\nThat practice — commissioning a personalised version of an existing rhythm — is what produced the enormous Jamaican tradition of multiple vocalists over the same instrumental.\n\nThe convention travelled with the diaspora. UK jungle, garage, and grime all adopted dubplate culture wholesale, with producers cutting exclusives for specific DJs and MCs recording custom versions.\n\nDigital distribution has removed the physical constraint without removing the social one. Exclusives still circulate, they just no longer wear out.",["Exclusivity, not fidelity, was the point","The practice migrated to UK garage and grime decades later"],"turntable","story",{}),
("04-17","'Superstition' began as a gift to a guitarist","Soul","1972","Stevie Wonder wrote the clavinet riff for Jeff Beck. When Motown heard it, they insisted Wonder keep it, straining the friendship.\n\nThe arrangement had been informal: Beck would play on Wonder's Talking Book sessions, and Wonder would write him a song in return. He came up with the clavinet riff more or less on the spot, reportedly while Beck was in the room playing a drum pattern.\n\nThe clavinet is the key to the record. It is an electric keyboard whose strings are struck and immediately damped, producing a short, plucked, percussive attack that behaves more like a rhythm guitar than a piano — which is precisely why a guitarist's song works without a guitar on it.\n\nWonder played essentially everything: drums, clavinet, Moog bass, and the horn arrangement written over it.\n\nWhen Motown heard the finished track they refused to let it go, correctly identifying it as a hit. Beck was, by all accounts, unhappy. He did record his own version with Beck, Bogert & Appice, but it came out after Wonder's had already reached number one, which rather defeated the purpose.\n\nThe two remained on speaking terms. Beck described the situation, years later, with a resignation that suggests he had never entirely got over it.",["Wonder played drums, clavinet and moog bass himself","Beck did record a version, but it came out after Wonder's"],"piano","story",{}),
("04-18","The producer who never let artists hear playback","Pop","1963","Phil Spector often refused to let performers hear the full mix, keeping the arrangement in his head so no one could argue with it.\n\nThe control was total by design. Sessions at Gold Star ran for hours with dozens of musicians playing doubled and tripled parts, and the only person who knew what the finished record would sound like was Spector. Singers frequently cut vocals without hearing the assembled arrangement, which removed any basis on which they could object to it.\n\nThat method produced records of genuine power and a working environment that was, by many first-hand accounts, controlling and frightening well before it became criminal.\n\nDarlene Love is the clearest case. Her voice is on records credited to the Crystals and to Bob B. Soxx and the Blue Jeans, and Spector released her performances under whichever group name suited him commercially. She spent years in litigation over royalties and credit and was at one point cleaning houses while hearing her own uncredited voice on the radio at Christmas.\n\nSpector was convicted of the second-degree murder of Lana Clarkson in 2009 and died in prison in 2021.\n\nThe records remain in the canon. The people who made them were treated as raw material by a man whose method depended on them not knowing what they were making.",["Sessions ran for hours with dozens of players","Darlene Love's voice appeared on records credited to other groups"],"mixer","producer",{}),
("04-19","'Apache' is called the national anthem of hip-hop","Funk","1973","The Incredible Bongo Band's cover gave DJs a long percussion break perfect for cutting between two turntables.\n\nThe song began as a British instrumental hit for the Shadows in 1960. The Incredible Bongo Band — a loose Los Angeles studio project — recorded a cover in 1973 that nobody paid much attention to at the time, and it contains a long percussion passage built around congas and bongos with almost no melodic content.\n\nThat passage is what mattered. A break needs to be rhythmically strong and harmonically neutral so a DJ can loop it indefinitely without the ear tiring of a chord sequence, and this one runs longer than most, which makes cutting between two copies considerably easier.\n\nKool Herc played it at Bronx block parties in the mid-seventies, and it became a foundational record for the whole form — sampled by the Sugarhill Gang, Nas, LL Cool J, and hundreds of others, and used as the backing for countless routines.\n\nThe phrase national anthem of hip-hop attaches to it because for a period in the seventies it functioned as one: the record everyone played, that everyone recognised, that reliably filled a floor.\n\nAlmost nobody involved in making it had any idea what it would become.",["Kool Herc played it at Bronx block parties","The break is essentially a drum solo with congas"],"drums","sample",{"src":"Incredible Bongo Band - Apache (1973)","hits":["Sugarhill Gang - Apache","Nas - Made You Look","LL Cool J - You Can't Dance"]}),
("04-20","Luther Vandross sang jingles before he sang ballads","R&B","1975","Vandross earned a living singing commercials and backing vocals for Bowie and Chic before launching a solo career.\n\nThe session and jingle world was, for a long time, where the best singers in New York actually made their money. It paid reliably, it was anonymous, and it demanded a specific skill — the ability to deliver a perfect take quickly, blend with anyone, and sing whatever was in front of you without ego.\n\nVandross was exceptional at it. He sang on commercials for soft drinks and fast food chains, did session work across the industry, and arranged the backing vocals on David Bowie's Young Americans — a substantial creative contribution on a landmark record, credited but not headlined. He also sang with Chic and appeared on a long list of other people's albums.\n\nThe solo career took years to start because the industry had him filed as a background voice, which is a common trap: being excellent at supporting work makes you too useful to release.\n\nWhen he did break through, the voice that had been trained by all that session discipline turned out to be one of the great instruments in R&B — extraordinarily controlled, with a sense of phrasing that comes from years of serving somebody else's song.\n\nHe died in 2005, at fifty-four.",["He arranged the backing vocals on Bowie's 'Young Americans'","He sang on ads for soft drinks and fast food chains"],"mic","story",{}),
("04-21","Prince died, leaving a vault of unreleased music","Funk","2016","Prince recorded relentlessly, storing thousands of unreleased tracks in a bank-style vault at Paisley Park. Estimates suggest decades of material.\n\nThe vault is literal — a climate-controlled room with a bank-style door at Paisley Park, containing tapes going back to the seventies. Estimates of its contents vary wildly, but people who have been inside describe enough finished and near-finished material to release albums for decades.\n\nThe working method explains the volume. He recorded alone through the night, playing every part and frequently engineering himself, and he did it continuously for forty years. Finished songs were shelved not because they were weak but because he had already moved on.\n\nHe also gave enormous amounts away. 'Nothing Compares 2 U', 'Manic Monday', and 'I Feel for You' were written by him and became hits for other people, in some cases before most listeners knew who had written them.\n\nHe died in April 2016 of an accidental fentanyl overdose, having been treated for chronic hip pain for years, and left no will — which produced a long and expensive estate dispute before any of the vault material could be released.\n\nThe posthumous releases have been careful. Whether he would have sanctioned any of them is unknowable and was, throughout his life, the thing he cared most about controlling.",["He often recorded alone overnight, engineering himself","Some vault songs date to the seventies"],"guitar","story",{}),
("04-22","Which 'Girls Just Want to Have Fun' wins: Hazard or Lauper?","Pop","1979","Robert Hazard wrote it from a man's perspective as a demo. Cyndi Lauper rewrote the meaning entirely, turning it into a feminist anthem.\n\nHazard's demo is sung by a man who is pleased that girls want to have fun, which is a different proposition entirely — the women in it are objects of the narrator's satisfaction rather than subjects of their own.\n\nLauper's initial reaction was refusal. She disliked the song and did not want to record it, and only agreed after reworking the lyric so that the perspective shifted to the women themselves. The changes are not extensive on the page and they relocate the song completely: it becomes a statement about autonomy made by the people it concerns.\n\nThe arrangement supports the reading. It is bright, fast, and slightly chaotic, and Lauper's vocal is deliberately unpolished in a way that reads as a person rather than a product.\n\nThe video did comparable work, casting her mother and featuring a visibly diverse cast at a point when MTV was not notable for either.\n\nIt is one of the clearest examples of a performer's interpretation constituting authorship. The notes are largely Hazard's. What the song means is Lauper's, and the meaning is the reason anyone still plays it.",["Lauper initially disliked the song and refused to record it","She rewrote lyrics to flip the point of view"],"mic","versus",{"a":"Robert Hazard (1979)","b":"Cyndi Lauper (1983)"}),
("04-23","Ray Charles fused gospel and blues and offended both","Soul","1954","'I Got a Woman' set secular lyrics to a gospel structure. Churches called it sacrilege; it also invented soul music.\n\nThe specific transgression was structural rather than vague. Charles took the harmonic and rhythmic architecture of a gospel song — reportedly reworking 'It Must Be Jesus' — and put lyrics about a woman over it, complete with the call-and-response phrasing, the melisma, and the ecstatic delivery that belonged, in the understanding of the time, to worship.\n\nChurches were furious, and the objection was not unreasonable on its own terms: the vocabulary of religious ecstasy was being used to describe something else entirely, and the resemblance was the point.\n\nRadio did not know what to do with it either. It was too raw for pop, too worldly for gospel, and not quite blues.\n\nWhat it created was soul music, which is essentially that fusion made permanent. Everything from Aretha Franklin to Otis Redding to Al Green runs through the door Charles opened, and most of those artists came out of church themselves.\n\nHe went on to do the same thing to country music a decade later with Modern Sounds in Country and Western Music, which offended a different set of people and sold enormously.\n\nHis instinct throughout was that genre boundaries were commercial fictions.",["He rewrote an existing gospel song's changes","Radio programmers were unsure which format it belonged to"],"piano","story",{}),
("04-24","Kool Herc invented the breakbeat at a Bronx party","Hip-Hop","1973","Herc noticed dancers waited for the drum break, so he used two copies of a record to loop it indefinitely. Hip-hop starts there.\n\nThe observation came first. Playing records at parties, he noticed that the floor filled during the short percussion passage in the middle of a funk record and emptied when the vocals returned. The breaks were where the dancing actually happened, and they lasted about ten seconds.\n\nThe solution was mechanical. Two copies of the same record on two turntables, cueing the second back to the start of the break while the first played, then switching — extending ten seconds into as long as he wanted. He called it the merry-go-round.\n\nEverything else follows from that. Dancers who specialised in the break became b-boys. The MC's role grew out of talking over the extended instrumental. Sampling is the technological version of the same idea.\n\nThe party in question, on 11 August 1973 at 1520 Sedgwick Avenue, was a back-to-school fundraiser organised by his sister Cindy Campbell to buy clothes. It is as good a founding date as any genre has.\n\nHerc was born in Kingston and brought Jamaican sound system practice with him, which is the direct line connecting Caribbean street culture to the Bronx.\n\nHe has never made significant money from any of it.",["He called it the 'merry-go-round' technique","The party was a back-to-school fundraiser for his sister"],"turntable","story",{}),
("04-25","Ella and Louis recorded almost entirely live","Jazz","1956","Their duet albums were cut with both singers in the room, no overdubs, letting the timing breathe imperfectly on purpose.\n\nThe two voices could hardly be more different. Fitzgerald's is precise, agile, and immaculately in tune; Armstrong's is a gravelled rasp with limited range and a sense of time that pulls against the beat constantly. On paper the pairing should not work.\n\nWhat makes it work is the room. Both singers were recorded together, live, without overdubs, which means every response is a real response — the small hesitations, the overlaps, and the moments where one clearly amuses the other are events rather than edits.\n\nProducer Norman Granz kept takes with audible laughter in them, which was not standard practice and is most of why the records feel like eavesdropping rather than listening.\n\nThe rhythm section was Oscar Peterson's trio with Buddy Rich on drums, playing with unusual restraint. Armstrong's trumpet appears throughout, and his playing is the other half of his contribution — by 1956 the voice had worn considerably and the horn had not.\n\nModern recording would fix everything that makes these records what they are. Both parts would be cut separately, tuned, and aligned, and the result would be cleaner and completely inert.",["Producer Norman Granz kept takes with audible laughter","The rhythm section was Oscar Peterson's trio"],"horn","story",{}),
("04-26","Grandmaster Flash built his mixer by hand","Hip-Hop","1977","Trained in electronics, Flash rigged a crossfader and cue system so he could hear the next record before the crowd did.\n\nThe problem he was solving was precise. Kool Herc's technique required switching between two copies of a record at exactly the right moment, and there was no way to check where the second record was without the audience hearing it. Herc did it by eye and by feel, and it was frequently approximate.\n\nFlash had studied electronics at a vocational school, and he wired a preview system — a single-pole double-throw switch feeding a headphone amp — that let him cue the second turntable silently. Parts were salvaged from junked stereos and car audio.\n\nThat single capability is the foundation of everything DJs do now. Being able to hear the next record before the crowd is what makes accurate beat-matching possible, and once it was possible, mixing became a craft with a technical standard rather than an approximation.\n\nHe built the rest of the vocabulary on top of it: backspinning, punch phrasing, and the clock theory of marking a record so he could find a specific point instantly.\n\nThe crossfader as a standard feature on DJ mixers exists because manufacturers eventually copied what he and his contemporaries had already built for themselves.",["He soldered parts salvaged from junked stereos","That preview capability made beat-matching possible"],"mixer","producer",{}),
("04-27","'Change the Beat' gave the world its most-scratched sound","Hip-Hop","1982","The 'ahhh, fresh' vocoder phrase at the end of Fab 5 Freddy's record became the single most-scratched sample in DJ history.\n\nThe phrase lasts under two seconds. It was recorded through a vocoder at the very end of the B-side, apparently as an afterthought when there was tape left, and by most accounts was spoken by Beeside — the French rapper on the record — rather than Fab 5 Freddy himself.\n\nWhat made it the standard is acoustic. The sound has a sharp transient at the front and a sustained vowel behind it, which is exactly what a scratch needs: something that speaks clearly when the record is moved a short distance and stays legible when the direction reverses. Turntablists tested a great deal of material over the years and kept coming back to this.\n\nIt is almost certainly the most-scratched sample in existence. It appears in Herbie Hancock's 'Rockit', in an enormous proportion of battle routines, and on records across five decades.\n\nDJ battles are judged on technique, and having a common reference sound means competitors can be compared directly — which turned a throwaway two seconds into something closer to a standard tuning note.\n\nFab 5 Freddy, a Brooklyn artist, was also the bridge between the downtown art world and the uptown hip-hop scene.",["It was recorded almost as an afterthought at the session's end","Nearly every turntablist routine still uses it"],"turntable","sample",{"src":"Fab 5 Freddy - Change the Beat (1982)","hits":["Countless DJ routines","Herbie Hancock - Rockit","Battle DJ competitions worldwide"]}),
("04-28","Ann Peebles wrote a hit about wanting bad weather","Soul","1973","'I Can't Stand the Rain' came from an offhand complaint about going out in a storm. Her co-writers turned the line into the hook on the spot.\n\nThe origin is as ordinary as it sounds. Peebles was heading out for the evening, it was raining, and she said she could not stand the rain. Her partner Don Bryant and the broadcaster Bernard 'Bernie' Miller recognised a hook in the sentence immediately, and the three of them wrote the song that night.\n\nThe production is the reason it endures. The opening sound — a repeating high electric timbale figure imitating individual raindrops — was played on a Univox electric timbale, and it establishes the entire mood before a word is sung. Willie Mitchell produced it at Hi Records in Memphis, using the same house band and the same warm, understated approach that made Al Green's records.\n\nJohn Lennon is widely quoted as calling it the best song he had ever heard, which he said on the radio and which did the record no harm at all.\n\nIt was covered repeatedly, sampled by Missy Elliott on 'The Rain', and has had a longer afterlife than its modest chart placing suggested.\n\nPeebles was a genuinely powerful singer whose career was quieter than her talent warranted. She died in 2024.",["John Lennon called it the best song he'd ever heard","The opening electric timbale imitates falling rain"],"drums","writer",{}),
("04-29","Duke Ellington wrote for specific players, not instruments","Jazz","1899","Ellington composed around individual musicians' quirks, writing parts only they could play. Replacing a member meant rewriting the music.\n\nThis is the thing that separates him from every other composer of comparable stature. A conventional score specifies trumpet; Ellington's specified Cootie Williams, or Bubber Miley, or Johnny Hodges — and the part exploited that particular player's tone, growl technique, or way of bending a note. He described his orchestra as his instrument, and he meant it literally.\n\nThe consequence is that the band was not replaceable. When a member left, the music written for them either left with him or had to be rewritten, and Ellington's players consequently stayed for decades, which is unheard of in a business where sidemen rotate constantly.\n\nIt also means the recordings are the compositions in a way that written scores are not. Later performances by other orchestras are, unavoidably, arrangements of something that was never fully notated.\n\nHe composed constantly and largely on the move — on trains between engagements, in hotel rooms, on the back of whatever paper was available — across a career spanning fifty years and thousands of pieces.\n\nBilly Strayhorn was his writing partner for nearly three decades, and the division of authorship between them remains genuinely difficult to untangle.",["He called his orchestra his real instrument","He wrote on trains between gigs for decades"],"horn","writer",{}),
("04-30","The Ditmas Park porch concert circuit","Brooklyn","2010","Neighbors around Cortelyou Road turned Victorian porches into micro-venues, hosting acoustic sets for audiences of thirty. The tradition continues each summer.\n\nThe format works because of the architecture. The blocks around Cortelyou were laid out around 1900 as freestanding wood-frame houses with deep front porches set close to the sidewalk — a design intended to encourage exactly this kind of semi-public sociability, a century before anyone thought to book a band on one.\n\nA porch is a genuinely good small venue. It is raised, so sightlines work; it is covered, so weather is survivable; and the audience stands on a lawn and a sidewalk, which means passers-by become part of the crowd without having to decide to attend.\n\nThe scale is the appeal. Thirty people, no PA or a very small one, no ticket, and a hat passed. Nothing about it scales up, which is the point — it produces the kind of listening that a room of a thousand cannot.\n\nSome porches host annual multi-act bills that have been running for years and are publicised almost entirely by word of mouth and neighbourhood mailing lists.\n\nThe lineage runs back through house-party culture across Flatbush and, further, to rent parties and stoop gatherings. Cortelyou's version is the same instinct with better woodwork.",["Some porches host annual multi-act bills","The format traces back to house-party culture across Flatbush"],"crowd","story",{}),
]
